{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    "module": "ESNext",
    /*
      DOM and nothing else: `src/` is userscript-side code, so the absence of `node` in `types` is itself a
      fact being checked — a consumer can compile this example without ever installing Node types.
    */
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM"],
    "types": ["xiv-datamine-polyfill/client"],
    /*
      What this project judges is the consumer's view: every import resolves through the checked package's
      `exports`, the wildcard `.csv` declaration is there to be found, and what it gives back feeds
      `useSheetTable`. Those three surface in `src/`, and they fail this compile whatever this flag says — a
      declaration that is missing reports `TS2307` at the import, one of the wrong shape `TS2345` at the call.
      Whether the declarations the packages ship are internally sound is not this project's question; that is
      `xiv-datamine-polyfill`'s `tsconfig.declarations.json`, which reads both packages' shipped declarations.
      Reading a `.d.ts` without judging it also keeps third-party declarations out of the verdict here.
    */
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noEmit": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"]
}
